B.ra = [
{id:"ra01", topic:"sleep", text:"Sleep is not simply a period of rest, but an active process during which the brain organises memories and repairs tissue. Researchers have found that students who sleep well perform better in examinations, while chronic sleep loss reduces attention, weakens immunity, and increases the risk of long-term health problems."},
{id:"ra02", topic:"urban planning", text:"As cities grow, planners face the challenge of providing housing without destroying green space. One popular solution is to build upwards rather than outwards, concentrating residents near public transport. This approach reduces car dependence, lowers emissions, and preserves farmland on the edges of urban areas."},
{id:"ra03", topic:"language learning", text:"Learning a second language in adulthood is often considered harder than in childhood, yet adults possess advantages of their own. They can use analytical skills, understand grammar rules explicitly, and set clear goals. Consistent daily practice, rather than occasional long sessions, appears to be the most reliable predictor of progress."},
{id:"ra04", topic:"renewable energy", text:"Solar and wind power have become cheaper than coal in many regions, which has transformed the energy debate. However, because the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow, storage technology remains essential. Better batteries will determine how quickly societies can rely on renewable sources."},
{id:"ra05", topic:"memory", text:"Human memory is not a perfect recording of past events. Each time we recall an experience, the brain reconstructs it, sometimes adding details that were never there. This explains why two witnesses can describe the same incident differently, and why confident memories are not always accurate ones."},
{id:"ra06", topic:"libraries", text:"Modern university libraries have changed considerably over the past two decades. Although printed books are still available, much of the collection is now digital, and students use the buildings as collaborative study spaces. Librarians have also become teachers, guiding students in how to evaluate sources and avoid plagiarism."},
{id:"ra07", topic:"oceans", text:"The oceans absorb a large share of the heat and carbon dioxide produced by human activity, which slows the pace of climate change on land. This service comes at a cost, however. Warmer, more acidic water damages coral reefs and threatens the fish populations on which millions of people depend."},
{id:"ra08", topic:"economics", text:"Inflation describes a general rise in prices over time, which reduces the purchasing power of money. Moderate inflation is usually considered healthy because it encourages spending and investment. Problems arise when prices rise faster than wages, leaving households unable to afford essential goods such as food and housing."},
{id:"ra09", topic:"education", text:"Many educators argue that exams measure only a narrow range of abilities. Projects, presentations, and group work can reveal creativity and teamwork, qualities that employers value highly. Nevertheless, supporters of traditional testing point out that it offers a fair and consistent way to compare large numbers of students."},
{id:"ra10", topic:"archaeology", text:"Archaeologists rarely find complete objects. More often they discover fragments of pottery, bone, and stone, which must be carefully cleaned, catalogued, and compared. By studying where these pieces were found and how they were made, researchers can reconstruct the daily lives of communities that left no written records."},
{id:"ra11", topic:"nutrition", text:"A balanced diet includes a variety of foods rather than large quantities of any single one. Fruit, vegetables, whole grains, and proteins each provide different nutrients that the body cannot produce on its own. Nutritionists warn that extreme diets, although popular, are difficult to maintain and may lead to deficiencies."},
{id:"ra12", topic:"technology", text:"Artificial intelligence is increasingly used to analyse medical images, detect fraud, and translate languages. While these applications save time and reduce costs, they also raise questions about privacy and accountability. Society must decide who is responsible when an automated system makes a serious mistake."},
{id:"ra13", topic:"psychology", text:"Motivation can be divided into two broad types. Intrinsic motivation comes from enjoyment of the task itself, while extrinsic motivation depends on rewards or fear of punishment. Studies suggest that people who study out of genuine curiosity tend to learn more deeply and persist longer when difficulties arise."},
{id:"ra14", topic:"transport", text:"Cycling is one of the most efficient forms of transport, requiring little space and producing no emissions. Cities that invest in protected bicycle lanes often see a rise in the number of commuters who choose to ride. As a result, traffic congestion falls and public health improves."},
{id:"ra15", topic:"biodiversity", text:"Biodiversity refers to the variety of living things in a particular habitat. A diverse ecosystem is generally more resilient, because if one species declines, others can fill its role. The loss of forests and wetlands therefore threatens not only wildlife but also the stability of the services that humans rely on."},
{id:"ra16", topic:"history of writing", text:"The invention of writing allowed knowledge to be stored outside the human mind. Early societies used marks on clay tablets to record trade and taxes, and only later did people begin to write stories and laws. This development made large and complex civilisations possible."},
{id:"ra17", topic:"workplace", text:"Remote work has altered the relationship between employees and their offices. Many workers appreciate the flexibility and the time saved on commuting. However, managers report that spontaneous conversations, which often lead to new ideas, are harder to reproduce through video calls."},
{id:"ra18", topic:"astronomy", text:"Telescopes allow astronomers to observe objects so distant that their light has travelled for billions of years. In effect, looking deeper into space means looking further back in time. This remarkable feature helps scientists understand how galaxies formed and how the universe has changed."},
{id:"ra19", topic:"water", text:"Access to clean water remains a serious problem in parts of the world. Contaminated supplies spread disease, and collecting water from distant sources takes time that children could spend in school. Simple measures, such as protecting wells and building small filtration systems, can produce significant benefits at low cost."},
{id:"ra20", topic:"sociology", text:"Social media has changed the way people form and maintain friendships. Users can stay in touch with relatives overseas and discover communities that share their interests. On the other hand, constant comparison with others may damage self-esteem, particularly among teenagers."},
{id:"ra21", topic:"agriculture", text:"Farmers around the world are adopting precision agriculture, which uses sensors and satellite data to apply water and fertiliser only where needed. This technique increases yields while reducing waste and pollution. Its main limitation is the initial cost, which many small farms find difficult to afford."},
{id:"ra22", topic:"linguistics", text:"Languages are constantly changing. New words appear as technology develops, while older expressions fall out of use. Linguists regard this process as natural rather than harmful, since it shows that a language is alive and adapting to the needs of its speakers."},
{id:"ra23", topic:"architecture", text:"Sustainable buildings are designed to use less energy throughout their lives. Architects achieve this by improving insulation, using natural light, and choosing materials with a low environmental impact. Although such features may raise construction costs, they typically reduce bills and pay for themselves over time."},
{id:"ra24", topic:"medicine", text:"Vaccination is one of the greatest public health achievements of the last two centuries. By training the immune system to recognise a pathogen, vaccines prevent illness before it begins. High rates of immunisation also protect those who cannot be vaccinated, because the disease finds it harder to spread."},
{id:"ra25", topic:"time management", text:"Effective students rarely rely on last-minute effort, because it seldom produces their best work. Instead, they divide large assignments into smaller tasks and assign each one a deadline. This habit reduces stress, improves the quality of the final work, and leaves time to revise before submission."},
{id:"ra26", topic:"geology", text:"The surface of the Earth is divided into enormous plates that move slowly over millions of years. Where these plates collide, mountains rise and earthquakes occur. Geologists monitor the boundaries carefully, but predicting the exact timing of an earthquake remains beyond current science."},
{id:"ra27", topic:"tourism", text:"Tourism brings income to many communities, yet it can also place pressure on local resources. Popular destinations may suffer from overcrowding, rising rents, and damage to historic sites. Some governments now limit visitor numbers or charge entry fees to protect the places that people come to see."},
{id:"ra28", topic:"economics", text:"Globalisation has connected markets, allowing goods and ideas to travel quickly across borders. Consumers benefit from lower prices and a wider choice. Critics argue, however, that local industries may struggle to compete, and that the gains from trade are not always shared equally."},
{id:"ra29", topic:"literacy", text:"Reading for pleasure is strongly associated with academic success, according to many studies. Children who read regularly develop larger vocabularies and stronger concentration. Teachers therefore encourage families to set aside a few minutes each evening for books, even when schedules are busy."},
{id:"ra30", topic:"climate", text:"Cities are usually warmer than the surrounding countryside, a phenomenon known as the urban heat island effect. Concrete and asphalt absorb heat during the day and release it at night. Planting trees and installing reflective roofs can lower temperatures and make summers more comfortable."},
{id:"ra31", topic:"neuroscience", text:"The adult brain remains adaptable throughout life, a property called neuroplasticity. When people learn a new skill, connections between neurons strengthen and reorganise. This discovery has changed medical practice, because patients recovering from injury can often regain lost abilities through repeated training."},
{id:"ra32", topic:"marketing", text:"Successful advertising depends on understanding the audience. Companies gather information about what customers need and how they make decisions, then design messages that address those concerns. Trust is equally important, since consumers are quick to ignore claims that appear exaggerated."},
{id:"ra33", topic:"sport science", text:"Regular exercise improves not only physical fitness but also mental health. Moderate activity, such as brisk walking for thirty minutes, can reduce anxiety and improve concentration. Doctors therefore recommend that adults include movement in their daily routine, whatever their age."},
{id:"ra34", topic:"waste", text:"Recycling reduces the amount of rubbish sent to landfill and saves raw materials. Its success, however, depends on correct sorting by households. When food or liquids contaminate paper and plastic, entire batches may be rejected, so clear labelling and public education are essential."},
{id:"ra35", topic:"philosophy of science", text:"A scientific theory is not simply a guess. It is an explanation supported by repeated observation and experiment, and it must be capable of being proved wrong. When new evidence contradicts a theory, scientists revise it, which is why science progresses over time."},
{id:"ra36", topic:"urbanisation", text:"Over half of the world's population now lives in cities, and this proportion is expected to rise. Rapid growth puts pressure on housing, schools, and sanitation. Governments that plan ahead for infrastructure are better placed to ensure that new residents enjoy a decent standard of living."},
{id:"ra37", topic:"museums", text:"Museums are no longer just buildings that store old objects. Many now offer interactive exhibits, online collections, and educational programmes for schools. By making their material accessible to a wider audience, they hope to inspire curiosity about history, art, and science."},
{id:"ra38", topic:"finance", text:"Saving money early in life has a powerful effect because of compound interest, in which earnings themselves begin to generate further earnings. Even small monthly deposits can grow substantially over several decades. Financial advisers therefore urge young people to start saving as soon as they receive an income."},
{id:"ra39", topic:"communication", text:"Effective communication involves listening as much as speaking, although this is often forgotten. Good listeners pay attention, avoid interrupting, and ask questions to confirm their understanding. In academic and professional settings, these habits prevent misunderstandings and help build cooperation between colleagues."},
{id:"ra40", topic:"public health", text:"Air pollution is linked to respiratory disease, heart problems, and reduced life expectancy. The main sources include vehicle exhaust, industrial emissions, and the burning of fuel for heating. Cleaner transport and stricter regulation of factories can bring measurable improvements in a surprisingly short time."}
];
